---
import Answer from "../../components/challenge/Answer.astro";
import ChallengeIntro from "../../components/challenge/ChallengeIntro.astro";
import Checklist from "../../components/challenge/Checklist.astro";
import Message from "../../components/challenge/Message.astro";
import Task from "../../components/challenge/Task.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="Reference Search - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="exploring-ai-capabilities" subChapterId="regex">
		<ChallengeIntro skill="Some work is easy for an agent and hard for people: a regex with many edge cases, and the unit tests that prove it.">
			The claims team wants one search box in the admin portal that understands reference numbers however a customer reads them
			out.
		</ChallengeIntro>

		<Message kind="ticket" id="CLM-212" type="Story" priority="Medium" reporter="Claims team lead" title="One search box for customer reference numbers">
			<p>
				Customers read their reference numbers to us over the phone and they never say them the same way. "hm 204817", "it's
				PH00412", "my claim is CL 4F2K…". Can we get one search box in the admin portal where we paste whatever they said and it
				takes us straight to the right record?
			</p>
		</Message>

		<Task>Write a prompt to add the reference search to the admin portal, with unit tests that prove the matching works.</Task>

		<Checklist
			items={[
				"Add a search box to every admin portal page that goes to the matching claim, policy, policyholder or quote.",
				"Cope with lowercase, spaces, dots or no separator, and a reference inside a sentence.",
				"Say so when the text isn't a reference, instead of showing an empty table.",
				"Match every reference format the app produces, not only the ones in the seed data.",
				"Keep the matching in one function, with unit tests for what should and shouldn't match.",
			]}
		/>

		<Answer
			prompt={`Add a search box to every admin portal page where staff paste a reference a customer gave them, in whatever format, and it goes to the right record with the table narrowed to that row.

Check how ids are generated in the API, not just the seed data, so every format matches. Put the matching in one function with unit tests that bun test runs, covering what should match and what shouldn't. Ask me about any case where guessing could open the wrong record.`}
		>
			<p>
				The trap is that claims and quotes come in two formats, and the ticket doesn't say so. Seeded rows have older numeric ids
				of different lengths, such as <code>CL-54871</code> and <code>HM-204817</code>. New claims and quotes get 8 characters
				from an alphabet without look-alikes, from <code>newId</code> in the API's <code>schema.ts</code>. An agent that only
				reads the seed writes a digits-only regex, and new claims and quotes stop matching. File a quote on the website or a
				claim in the consumer portal to get a new id to test with.
			</p>
			<p>Cases worth seeing in the tests:</p>
			<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-1">
				<li>Separators: <code>hm 204817</code>, <code>HM.204817</code>, <code>hm204817</code>.</li>
				<li>Inside a sentence: <code>my claim is cl-54871 thanks</code>.</li>
				<li>
					Must not match: <code>phone 555-0102</code>, <code>HMMM-1234</code>, a dollar amount, a date, and an 8-character id
					with a <code>0</code>, <code>O</code>, <code>1</code> or <code>I</code> in it.
				</li>
				<li>
					The edge of the formats: <code>CL-23456789</code> is a valid new id even though it's all digits, and{" "}
					<code>CL-12345678</code> is neither format.
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>
				A good question for the agent to raise: should <code>CL-5487I</code> be read as <code>CL-54871</code>? Customers do say
				"I" for "1", but guessing could open the wrong customer's claim. Either answer is fine if the tests pin it down.
			</p>
			<p>Payments have no admin portal page, so they're out of scope.</p>
		</Answer>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
